---
// Isometric "exploded layers" scene (v2, 19.07 — replaces the flying-cards
// story the owner rejected as cluttered). Four parallel plates — Background,
// Image, Content, Navigation — sit in one isometric stack and only ever move
// along the stack axis (translateZ), so every frame of the loop is a clean,
// composed exploded view: plates stay parallel and can never collide or pile
// up mid-flight. A scan beam sweeps through the stack and a screen-facing
// PSD badge anchors the output side of the story.
//
// Pure CSS (transform/opacity only), zero JS, zero CLS. Always decorative:
// parents wrap it in aria-hidden="true" and give it a fixed-size dark
// container (.ext-visual / .scene-band). Labels billboard back to screen
// orientation via inverse rotations (rotateZ then rotateX undo the iso
// wrapper's rotateX·rotateZ). prefers-reduced-motion freezes the stack in
// its exploded state with labels visible (see global.css).
---

<div class="scene">
  <div class="iso">
    <div class="plate p-bg"><span class="pl-tag">Background</span></div>
    <div class="plate p-img">
      <i class="el-img"></i>
      <span class="pl-tag">Image</span>
    </div>
    <div class="plate p-content">
      <i class="el-title"></i>
      <i class="el-copy"></i>
      <i class="el-copy c2"></i>
      <i class="el-btn"></i>
      <span class="pl-tag">Content</span>
    </div>
    <div class="plate p-nav">
      <i class="el-chrome"><b></b><b></b><b></b></i>
      <i class="el-navbar"></i>
      <span class="pl-tag">Navigation</span>
    </div>
    <div class="beam"></div>
  </div>
  <!-- Both REAL extension outputs, not just one: PSD Export produces a
       layered .psd, Site to Canva lands the capture in Canva. -->
  <div class="scene-out">
    <div class="out-card out-canva"><span>Canva</span></div>
    <div class="out-card out-psd"><span>PSD</span></div>
  </div>
</div>
